{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Lo yassur shevet miYehudah.</b> Once Yehudah begins to rule — from the kingdom of David onward — his dominion will not depart to be given to another tribe, as the kingdom of Sha'ul was removed and given to David." "<b>Umechoqeq miyn raglav.</b> So is the way of every scribe — to sit between the feet of the ruler." "<b>Ad ki yavo Shiloh.</b> For then the kingdom of the house of David was divided, and the kingdom of the ten tribes was given to Yerov'am; this is ״ad ki yavo Shiloh,״ as it says: they came to Shiloh and crowned Yerov'am over them. Another interpretation: ״ad ki yavo Shiloh״ — Ahiyah the Shilonite, who was from Shiloh, who tore Rehav'am's garment into twelve pieces." "<b>U-lo yikhat amim.</b> They gathered there to crown Yerov'am, as it is written: ״Va-yavo Rehav'am Shekhemah, ki sham ba kol Yisra'el lehamlikho״ (and Rehav'am came to Shekhem, for all Israel came there to make him king), and Shekhem and Shiloh are adjacent to each other, as it is written: ״Va-yesof Yehoshua et kol ha-am Shekhemah״ etc. (and Yehoshua gathered all the people to Shekhem), and immediately afterward it is written: ״Va-yanichehu sham be-mikdash HaShem״ (and he set it there in the sanctuary of HaShem), which is the tabernacle of Shiloh. And this peshat serves as a reply to the sectaries. And Ya'akov explained the essence of Yehudah's greatness — from David to Rehav'am — but the loss of greatness he did not wish to explain; yet from the implication of the verse you can infer that from Shiloh onward it was diminished."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:09:15.872099600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}